{"text": "You want to test whether score equals 100. Which line is correct Python?
if score = 100:
if score == 100:
if score is 100:
if score > 100 and score < 100:
Comparison uses == (two equals signs); a single = is assignment. A condition that asks whether two values are equal must be written score == 100. This directly confronts the misconception that `if score = 100:` uses one `=` to compare."}

{"text": "You want a block of code to run only when temp > 100 is false. Which if-else structure achieves that?
if temp > 100: print('boiling') else: print('not boiling')
if temp > 100: print('not boiling') else: print('boiling')
if temp > 100 and else: print('not boiling')
if temp > 100: print('not boiling')
In an if-else, one and only one branch is taken. The else branch runs when temp > 100 is false, so it prints 'not boiling' exactly then."}

{"text": "Which set is a subspace of R^3?
{(x,y,1) : x,y in R}
{(x,y,0) : x,y in R}
{(x,y,z) : x+y+z=1}
{(x,y,z) : x^2+y^2+z^2=1}
The set {(x,y,0)} contains the zero vector and is closed under addition and scaling, so it is a subspace. The other options fail the subspace tests. In particular, “Only lines and planes through the origin are subspaces; others miss the zero vector and are not closed under scaling.”"}

