{"text": "You want to print the numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. Which loop does that?
for i in range(5): print(i)
for i in range(1, 5): print(i)
for i in range(5, 0): print(i)
while i < 5: print(i)
`range(5) yields 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 — it starts at 0 and stops before the endpoint, giving exactly five values.` That is why this loop prints the five numbers you want. By contrast, `range(1, 5)` starts at 1, which is the misconception that `range(5)` means 1 through 5."}
